<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">State of Qin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century–<a href="/years/221-bce">221 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> · Also known as Qin (state)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> was a Zhou-era state in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-river">Wei River</a> valley of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. After the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shang-yang">Shang Yang</a> in the 4th century BCE it became the strongest military power of the Warring States, conquering Shu in 316 BCE and ending the Zhou royal line in 256 BCE. Between 230 and 221 BCE it destroyed the other six states and founded the Qin dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/state-of-qin.jpg" alt="State of Qin" width="320" height="295" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philg88 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EN-QIN260BCE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> is traditionally dated to 897 BCE and arose from a reconquest of western lands lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rong-people">Xirong</a>. Its position at the western edge of Chinese civilisation allowed an expansion unavailable to its rivals on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>'s Records of the Grand Historian, it traced its origin to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuanxu">Zhuanxu</a>, one of the Five Emperors of ancient times. Its ancestors, the Ying clan, served the Shang as a buffer against the Xirong. Under the Zhou, Feizi so impressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-xiao-of-zhou">King Xiao of Zhou</a> with his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-breeding">horse breeding</a> that he received a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> in the valley of Qin, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, with its seat at Qinyi. The Ying lived among the Rong tribes, sometimes fighting them and sometimes intermarrying with their kings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the Spring and Autumn period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> dealt mainly with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rong-people">Rong</a> to the west. Its main contact was its eastern neighbour Jin. When Jin suffered famine in 647 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-mu-of-qin">Duke Mu of Qin</a> sent relief, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-hui-of-jin">Duke Hui of Jin</a> gave none when Qin starved the next year. War followed in 645 BCE, and Duke Mu captured Duke Hui and released him in return for land and an alliance. He then backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-wen-of-jin">Chong'er</a>, who became Duke Wen of Jin, and turned to campaigns against the Rong.</p><p>In the early Warring States period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> was less involved in the Central Plains, apparently through an isolationist policy, and relied on natural defences such as the Hangu Pass. Between 413 and 409 BCE a Wei army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-qi">Wu Qi</a> took Qin territory west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>. In 362 BCE Qin defeated Wei and Han, and in 361 BCE Duke Xiao invited talented men from other states to assist his reforms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shang-yang">Shang Yang</a> carried out Legalist reforms against conservative opposition, abolishing direct <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">primogeniture</a>, granting commoners citizenship rights, resettling many to raise farm output and imposing strict laws that spared neither nobility nor royalty.</p><p>In 247 BCE the 13-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Ying Zheng</a> became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, and he took full power in 238 BCE after eliminating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-buwei">Lü Buwei</a> and Lao Ai. With help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-fei">Han Fei</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-si">Li Si</a> and Wei Liao he planned the conquest of the other six states, which unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 221 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>'s unification under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a> in 221 BCE founded the Qin dynasty, which, despite its short duration, influenced later Chinese history greatly. The state before that is called predynastic or proto-Qin. The Wuzi, attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-qi">Wu Qi</a>, described the Qin people as ferocious by nature, in a treacherous terrain, with strict and impartial decrees and clear rewards and punishments.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qin (state)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 506 BCE the State of Wu, advised by the Chu exile Wu Zixu, defeated Chu at Boju and occupied Ying; King Zhao fled, and the state survived only with help from the <b>State of Qin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The regional lords took a growing part in defence, and their armies, especially those of the State of Jin, the <b>State of Qin</b> and the State of Zheng, eventually outweighed the king's.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou · Military</a></li></ul></section>
